  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nCurrently observing elevated showers and storms across NE this \nafternoon, associated with a mid level perturbation trailing \nsoutheast across the central plains. Main question is if these \nstorms will dissipate or maintain themselves as they enter northern \nKansas after 2 PM. Upper forcing weakens by late afternoon before a \nstronger wave arrives late tonight. In the meantime, atmosphere in \nour northern counties continues to destabilize today as MUCAPE \nreaches 3000 J/KG in far northeast Kansas after 3 PM. Bulk shear \nincreases by early evening as well to near 50 kts. If storms \nmaintain themselves or redevelop, the atmosphere is supportive of \nsevere wind gusts and large hail. It's important to note that \nseveral cams, including the HRRR, have weakened storms before they \nenter our area so there exists higher than average uncertainty in \nstorm evolution this afternoon. Better chances for showers and \nstorms comes late tonight as a stronger upper trough develops \nanother storm complex over Nebraska. Track of these storms has been \nmost consistent impacting north central Kansas so have placed higher \npops there through Saturday morning. Severe probabilities are not as \nhigh with overnight convection, however cannot rule out isolated \nwind gusts to 60 mph and small hail.\n\nResidual showers and cloud cover should clear by late morning \nSaturday as a cooler sfc high slides southward during the afternoon. \nHighs are a tad lower in the mid to upper 80s, and may fall further \ninto the lower 80s Sunday afternoon, dependent on lingering showers \nand cloud cover. Ensemble guidance maintains a strong consensus for \nthe upper ridge to stay suppressed over the Southwest, leaving the \nCentral Plains within an active shortwave pattern Sunday through next \nweek. Maintained slight chance pops Sunday afternoon as a stronger \nvort max approaches the region, increasing pops overnight as the \nenhanced LLJ aids in a cluster of storms dropping southeast out of \nNE. \n\nAdditional upper disturbances within the northwest flow may bring \nrenewed shower and storm chances Tuesday evening and Thursday. \nTemperatures through next week are near normal for this time of year \nin the upper 80s to low 90s for highs.",
